   image gate: kinzie_house_1812 chicago_magazine_1857: verdict "differs", 1 difference(s) open: walls: the engraving draws them light and smooth-faced with a corner board, as weatherboard or whitewash reads, about 1833. The 1812 house is built of squared logs from Moses and Kirkland's text, and Lossing's 1812 view draws them dark and coursed; the plate is evidence of its own date, about twenty years later, and a cladding put on in the 1810s or 1820s is the likeliest reading. Not built for 1812
   image gate: kinzie_house_1812 cram_1893: verdict "differs", 2 difference(s) open: walls: drawn light and smooth, with a corner board at the west end, as weatherboard or whitewash reads, 'in 1832' as the caption says. The same reading as the 1857 engraving's, and the same ruling: the 1812 house is built of squared logs (Moses and Kirkland) as Lossing's 1812 view draws it, and the plate is evidence of its own date, twenty years later. Not built for 1812; piazza height: on the rebuilt piazza (2.95 m plate, Sarony's reading) every post's foot lands 3-7 px above the drawn foot and every head 3-6 px below the drawn head, so the plate still draws the piazza about 1.2 times as tall as the build (1.7 times before T-2516). Sarony's lithograph, drawn by a member of the household, governs the height
   image gate: kinzie_house_1812 eastman_1820: verdict "differs", 1 difference(s) open: walls: drawn smooth and light on the gable end, as weatherboard reads, in 1820; the build's are squared log. Eight years after the 1812 scene and the earliest picture of the house; the same reading as the 1857, Cram and Teich records, and the same ruling: the 1812 house is built of squared logs (Moses and Kirkland) and Lossing's 1812 view draws them coursed
   image gate: kinzie_house_1812 gemmell_1820: verdict "differs", 1 difference(s) open: length: the plate draws the house from its east gable to the end of its north eave over about 62 px (x 1087-1150), the build's runs to x 1167 from this camera, and the Teich painting and Sarony's lithograph also draw the front shorter against the end. Sarony's stacks land within 3 px on a 40 ft ridge, so the roof he drew is the build's length; at 40 px on a blurred scan this plate cannot overrule it, and the 40 ft length stands as its liberty records
   image gate: kinzie_house_1812 lossing_1869: verdict "differs", 4 difference(s) open: stacks: Lossing draws two through the ridge, at x 200 px (0.4 of the ridge from the west gable) and x 163 px (by the west gable). The build's middle stack lands at x 194 px on his x 200; its other stands by the EAST gable (x 242 px), where the 1857 engraving, the Teich postcard and the Cram plate put the second stack. Where they disagree, the 1857 engraving governs: it is twelve years earlier, made in Chicago, and draws the roof plainly; Lossing drew the house from a distance, more than fifty years after the fort burned; a dark mass behind the roof east of the stacks (x 240-280 px, y 250-270 px): it may be the far slope in shadow or a building behind. The 1857 engraving shows no range behind the house, so none is built; Wau-Bun's outbuildings and Ouilmette's and Pettell's cabins behind the house are T-2484's other pieces; piazza roof: Lossing carries the main roof down over the piazza in one plane, its eave at y 293 px. Rebuilt (T-2516) as the main roof carried down over the piazza, broken to a flatter pitch above the front wall: Sarony and Eastman draw that break close to, and it is the one roof that answers all five pictures. From this low eye the break shows where Lossing draws none; piazza height: Lossing's posts are about 32 px tall and the rebuilt ones (Sarony's 2.95 m plate) draw about 39 px, every head about 7 px above the drawn head; T-2497's 2.0 m posts matched him. Sarony's lithograph governs; Lossing drew the house from a distance more than fifty years after the fort burned
   image gate: kinzie_house_1812 sarony_1912: verdict "differs", 3 difference(s) open: length: the posts are drawn about 25 px apart where the build's 2.39 m bays draw 29-33 px from this camera, so the front reads about 0.85 of the build's 40 ft against its end, as the Teich painting and the Gemmell plate also draw it. The roof does not agree with the posts: both stacks, at 0.08 and 0.55 of a 40 ft ridge, land within 3 px of the drawn ones. So the 40 ft length (L-kinzie-house-1812-2497) stands and the spacing is read as the draughtsman's; walls: drawn light, smooth-faced and whitewashed, front and end; the standing ruling, which this ticket (T-2516) leaves alone: the 1812 house is built of squared logs (Moses and Kirkland), and a picture that draws the house light and smooth is evidence of a later cladding or whitewash. Not built for 1812; outbuildings and a two-storey house up the road to the west, behind the house: Wau-Bun's outbuildings and the neighbours' houses are T-2484's other pieces, not this record
   image gate: kinzie_house_1812 teich_postcard: verdict "differs", 3 difference(s) open: walls: the front under the piazza painted cream, as whitewash or weatherboard reads, the west end dark brown in shade; the build's are squared log throughout. The same reading and ruling as on the 1857 and Cram records: the painting shows the house as it was later (the card says 'first house built in Chicago', not a year), and the 1812 house is built of squared logs; west end: the small square window high in the gable (about 457, 285 px) is built (end_openings, after Sarony), and lands within about 8 px of the painted one. The end is still painted broader against the front than the build draws it (the apex 15 px left of the painted after the re-fit): a painter's perspective, and the 40 ft by 22 ft footprint stands (Sarony's stacks agree with it); roof colour: painted red-brown, as weathered shingle reads; the build's roof is the archetype's neutral grey-brown. The painting is the only picture in colour, its painter and date are not recorded and the postcard's colour printing is c. 1910's; Sarony's monochrome lithograph, which governs, cannot say, so no colour is taken from this one card
